{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1170399-card-based-initiative-in-cofd","id":1170399,"title":"Card based initiative in CofD","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-25T03:15:46-08:00","id":1170399,"title":"Card based initiative in CofD","content":"Any Savage Worlds fans in here?<br /> <br /> I am a big fan of the way Savage Worlds rpg uses normal playing cards for initiative. It is fast and very exciting in comparison to the standard initiative system CofD uses. So the goal of this hack is: remake the initiative system in order to make it faster yet more dynamic and complex at the same time.<br /> <br /> <i>Each round of combat </i>every player draws a new card and acts in the order indicated by the number of the card. The Storyteller does the same thing for her characters. If two or more cards of the same number show up, then let these go in alphabetical order according to colour. So Clubs --&gt; Diamonds --&gt; Hearts --&gt; Spades.<br /> <br /> Joker: Every time a Joker appears, it represents that character very much being in just the right place in the right moment in the ongoing chaos of combat. To represent this, the character with a Joker can act at any time in the round and may even disrupt the action of another character. The character also gains a +2 to all rolls. If the character causes damage from an attack they cause +2 damage. This applies even to supernatural actions.<br /> <br /> Everytime a Joker appears the deck of cards should be reshuffled again. In order to avoid delays because of this it may be a good idea to have two decks of cards and hand the recently used one to a player for shuffling and use the new one in the meantime.<br /> <br /> What about the Fast Reflexes Merit that grants initiative bonuses? I propose two new Merits, doing sort of similar things but in different ways and with different requirements:<br /> <br /> Quick Reflexes (3)<br /> Prerequisites: Dexterity 3, Composure 3<br /> This character is moving very quickly and reacts instantly to changes in their environment. Every time they draw an initiative card and get less than 5 they can draw more cards until they get a 5 or more.<br /> <br /> Level-headed (3 or 5)<br /> Prerequisites: Wits 3, Composure 3<br /> This character can think and process a situation very quickly and therefore act at the right time. With the 3-dot version of this Merit, at the start of every combat round, they draw two cards and may use the better. With the 5-dot Merit the character can draw 3 cards and choose the better.<br /> <br /> Can you have both of these Merits stacking with each other? Definitely. With both Merits at their maximum level the character draws 3 cards every round and if any of them are less than 5 the character keeps drawing them until they reach 5 or more.<br /> <br /> What about supernatural abilities that grant bonus to initiative? I propose this system:<br /> Any supernatural abilities that would give bonuses to Initiative instead allow the character to draw an appropriate number of cards beforehand and keep them to herself. When they then draw cards normally every combat round they may choose to instead use one of the cards they had drawn earlier, allowing them to go earlier than they otherwise would.<br /> <br /> Certain supernatural abilities allow the character to completely skip initiative altogether and act first of all. In this case this is exactly what happens, with the slight disadvantage that this character gets no Joker but can still act at any time in the round and also disrupt the actions of other players. If another character has a similar ability to go first, these two characters draw cards against each other before the other characters do. <hr /><br /> I would be very grateful for any constructive criticism of this. Do you think it is just too pulpy and against the themes of the game? Is there anything in CofD that makes this a bad idea?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-25T10:30:29-08:00","id":1170470,"title":"","content":"OK. I'm going to try not to be too negative here, but I think there's a lot of issues with trying to get this to work with the CofD system. To me these would be hurdles that would bar me from wanting to do it, but if they don't dissuade you and this might help you with solutions? Glad it might work for you.<br /> <br /> 1) Fast vs. dynamic via complexity. I really don't see where this kept things fast. The base concept from SW isn't bad speed-wise, but here it's all slowing things down. The card deck is a new gaming prop that's not used anywhere else, you're drawing every round instead of once at the start/maybe again if something big happens, and at the base level, combat doesn't become more dynamic (as in there's a sense of greater range of consequence) it simply becomes more random. When you start to add permutations and complexity to account for initiative modifying systems, all it does is push you back towards how the RAW CofD already works with certain characters easily getting higher Initiatives and those that aren't invested in or naturally capable of it are just rolling to see what order they go in.<br /> <br /> 2) Jokers are way too powerful, especially in combination with drawing multiple cards being the default way to modify your initiative (meaning that the odds of drawing a joker in a 54 setup are really high when a combat round could easily involve 20+ drawn cards). Any of the current bonuses are significant enough to be a high level Merit or low level supernatural power alone, and drawing a joker gets you all three with nothing but luck. Maybe have the player with a joker pick their bonus instead of that?<br /> <br /> 3) Character aptitude is no longer part of the calculation with this. That's one of the reason it tends to feel random over dynamic. In the RAW CofD system base initiative modifier even before Merits or powers is pretty important. It does not take that large of a difference to have vastly better odds of winning initiative, and ties are broken in the favor of character ability instead of luck (including Clash of Wills for power based ties). This has some important effects that your current idea doesn't address. It ensures that combat requires investment in a wider spread of Attributes to avoid one becoming too valuable to winning combat alone. It also has a dynamic interaction with weapon selection, as low initiative characters can basically take any weapon they want because they're not counting on going first, where high initiative characters have to consider a weapons initiative penalty more carefully.<br /> <br /> 4) The two proposed permutations (redraw until you hit an X or higher, and draw multiple cards) are insufficient to cover the RAW CofD systems. Two things of note that this doesn't deal with are initiative penalties, and initiative sacrificing (where you voluntarily reduce your initiative for some other bonus), because there's no built in negative below drawing one card. A standard 54 card deck is also incredibly small for this with how some of the games work. If you're playing Werewolf and you have five PCs in Urhan or Urshul, they're each drawing 3 cards, before the ST draws any, or any further Initiative boosts they might have access too. If they're fighting three enemy werewolves in Gauru, we're already at 21 cards just for the base bonuses for werewolf shapeshifting. And that's before anything like getting to draw multiple times until you eliminate lower cards, or someone that starts at a base of 3 cards instead of 1 via your proposed Merits. What do you do if some many cards are drawn, redrawing to get above a 4 is now impossible, but a character is supposed to be allow to do it?<br /> <br /> -----------<br /> <br /> Basically, it's not an issue of &quot;pulpy&quot; but random without any clear pay of for being more random. And it's just a very rough fit to account for the existing game mechanics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-14T05:32:11-08:00","id":1175961,"title":"","content":"I'm currently trying to figure a way to get to play Deadlands; Reloaded and implement the poker hands and cards in general to the CoD system, so far I couldn't find any conversion from the Savage Worlds system (non d20) to the Storytelling. Just in case I sounds confusing, I want to run a CoD campaign that it's based on a crossover universe with Deadlands while still keep it open to CoD cosmology, playing the Storytelling rule system and just adding up Cards and Poker Hands playing. Ideas?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23698-eien-hawkmoon","name":"Eien Hawkmoon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23698_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}